D. The eclipse dial, one cell a lunation (223 cells). Proxy for an eclipse: at new moon the Moon within 1.5
   deg of the ecliptic (a solar eclipse somewhere on Earth), at full moon within 1.0 deg (the Moon in the
   Earth's shadow). An ideal dial is engraved from the first 223 lunations after 1 May 205 BC.
   mean synodic month then 29.530589 d, now 29.530586 d; lunations from the first new moon then to the first now: 26647.995
   the ideal dial: 41 cells with a solar glyph, 29 with a lunar one, of 223
   Saros 1 after engraving ( 18.0 years on):  solar : 42 eclipses, 41 on a glyph cell (by chance  7.7);  lunar : 29 eclipses, 29 on a glyph cell (by chance  3.8)
   Saros 2 after engraving ( 36.1 years on):  solar : 42 eclipses, 40 on a glyph cell (by chance  7.7);  lunar : 30 eclipses, 29 on a glyph cell (by chance  3.9)
   Saros 3 after engraving ( 54.1 years on):  solar : 43 eclipses, 40 on a glyph cell (by chance  7.9);  lunar : 29 eclipses, 28 on a glyph cell (by chance  3.8)
